From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noxwell <beebono@yahoo.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 17:16:08 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 162/225] net: wireless: sc23xx: fix WiFi data-path throughput
 and stability
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three data-path fixes that together bring the SC2355 SDIO WiFi link to
line rate in both directions and stop it flapping under a reconnect loop.

RX A-MSDU loss (the big download fix). SC23XX_RX_LAST_MSDU was defined as
flags_0 BIT(1) = last_msdu_of_buff, which the firmware sets on essentially
every frame including each subframe of an A-MSDU. The BA reorder window
therefore advanced on the *first* subframe of an aggregate, and the real
trailing subframe(s) — sharing the same sequence number — arrived "old"
(in-order path) or "duplicate" (buffered/timeout path) and were dropped.
That lost ~one MSDU per A-MSDU under aggregation. Point it at the correct
bit, flags_0 BIT(5) = last_msdu_of_mpdu, matching the firmware's
rx_msdu_desc layout. Download over iperf3 goes 2.5 -> 176 Mbit/s, 0 retr;
single-MSDU frames also carry BIT(5) so non-aggregated traffic is
unaffected.

TX credit backpressure (the upload fix). On credit starvation the data
TX thread dropped the frame, which is a TCP loss signal that collapses
the window. Instead, requeue the frame and park; stop the netdev TX
queues at a backlog high-water mark and wake them when the thread drains
below the low-water mark, so the stack applies backpressure rather than
us dropping. The core kicks the parked thread via a new tx_kick bus op
when it replenishes credit. Upload goes ~5.8 -> 25 Mbit/s, 0 retr.

Power-state / reconnect flap. The TX thread declared HOST_IDLE at the top
of every loop and then parked on no-credit with frames still queued;
HOST_IDLE lets the firmware suspend its data subsystem and tear down the
RX path, dropping inbound frames like the DHCP OFFER -> "associated but
never gets an IP" -> ~46s reconnect/roam loop. Only go HOST_IDLE when the
backlog is truly empty; the moment there is work, go ACTIVE (waking the
firmware if suspended) before waiting on credit, so the CP keeps
delivering RX while we wait for a grant. Also wake the netdev queues on
connect/disconnect/roam, since queue state was otherwise only reset on
interface up/down and a roam could leave credit-backpressure wedged.

Also quiet the bring-up instrumentation (per-command / EAPOL / specdata /
tx-data logging) from wiphy_info to wiphy_dbg and drop the net_ratelimit()
wrappers around debug prints, which emitted "callbacks suppressed" spam
even with the dbg print itself disabled.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
